      "Language": "chi",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "OBJECTIVE",
          "content": "Whether proinsulin (PI) and true insulin (TI) are independently related to coronary artery disease (CAD)."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHODS",
          "content": "54 patients with CAD diagnosed by coronary angiography and/or intravascular ultrasound, 37 patients without CAD by coronary angiography and/or intravascular ultrasound and 23 healthy volunteers. All subjects had oral glucose tolerance test after an overnight 14 h fast. PI and TI concentrations were detected at different phases by BA-ELISA in which PI cross -reactivity is less than 0.1%."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "Fasting and post-glucose loading PI and TI levels in both CAD and non-CAD groups significantly increased when compared with the control group (P < 0.05), after adjustment for age, body mass index and waist hip ratio, all statistically significant associations weakened or disappeared among three groups; but there was no significant difference of all the above variables between CAD group and non-CAD group (P > 0.05)."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSION",
          "content": "PI and TI, whether fasting or post-glucose loading, may not be independently related to CAD."
        }
      ]},
